candidates.body:
Additional reproducers on main @ 2dbc40ee48fc7606c01538bf748911fba42c4177 that go beyond the ones in this issue:

1. The silent value is now a **wrap to int min**, not a clamp to int max, and some hex literals are accepted with a nonsensical value:
```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

public function main() {
    int x = 99999999999999999999; // @error
    int y = 0x8000000000000000; // @error
    io:println(x);
    io:println(y);
}
```
Actual: compiles and prints `-9223372036854775808` and `8000000000000000` (the latter is not even a valid int rendering). `9223372036854775808` (also as `const` or with `var`) gives `-9223372036854775808`, and `0x10000000000000000` prints `10000000000000000`.

2. The compiler crash already happens with a 16-digit (exactly 64-bit) hex literal:
```ballerina
public function main() {
    int x = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF; // @error
    _ = x;
}
```
Actual:
```
fatal[INTERNAL_ERROR]: failed to parse numeric literal
 --> bug3b-hex-literal-crash-e.bal:2:13
```
Expected: a normal out-of-range diagnostic in all of these cases. Spec §5.2.3: "The int type consists of integers between -9,223,372,036,854,775,808 and 9,223,372,036,854,775,807"; `HexIntLiteral` is an unsigned numeral with no two's-complement interpretation, and §6.3 selects int as the only candidate basic type when the expected type is int, so no valid value exists for these literals.
